4.4 Algorithm chunks
Check the heap invariant for every parent-child pair, not only the left child
ts-heap-invariant   lens: testing   level: L2   family: ts-heap-testing
Prerequisites: ts-heap-heapify. Follow-up: ts-heap-sort. Complexity: O(n).
 Common misconception. Comparing each parent with its left child is enough to validate a heap.
The invariant is that every parent is not larger than either child (for a min-heap). A check that tests only the left child
accepts arrays where the right child is smaller. Loop over all indexes i > 0 and compare with parent (i - 1) >> 1.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function isMinHeapBad(a: number[]): boolean {
  for (let i = 0; 2 * i + 1 < a.length; i++) {
    if ((a[i] as number) > (a[2 * i + 1] as number)) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
Correct:
 function isMinHeap(a: number[]): boolean {
  for (let i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    if ((a[(i - 1) >> 1] as number) > (a[i] as number)) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
Heapify builds a heap in O(n) by sifting down from the last parent to the root
ts-heap-heapify   lens: creation   level: L3   family: ts-heap-creation
Prerequisites: ts-heap-sift-down. Follow-up: ts-heap-invariant. Complexity: O(n).
 Common misconception. Sifting down from index 0 upwards builds a heap.
Sift-down assumes both subtrees are already heaps, so it must run on the lowest parents first. Start at the last parent,
index (n >> 1) - 1, and move toward 0. Running from 0 upward leaves deeper levels unfixed. The total work is O(n),
better than n pushes at O(n log n).
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
