Prompt. Why is building a heap with heapify O(n) while n pushes cost O(n log n)?
Answer key (mentor only, never embedded). Heapify sifts down from the last parent. Half of the nodes are leaves and
need no work, a quarter sift at most one level, an eighth at most two, and so on, so the total is the sum of h * n / 2^h,
which is O(n). Each push may sift up through log n levels, and there are n pushes. Use heapify when all values are
known up front.
